From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:56:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wscapi: Add the DLL, reporting the host's ClamAV as the
 antivirus.

Outlook asks Windows Security Center whether an antivirus is on and up
to date before it lets another program use its object model without
asking the user; with no wscapi.dll every script that read a mail item
got the "A program is trying to access e-mail address information"
prompt.

The antivirus Wine has is the host's: amsi.dll already hands what
programs ask it to scan to clamd.  So the Security Center reports that
one.  It is on when clamd answers on its socket, found as amsi finds
it, and its signatures are up to date for a week after they were
built, when ClamAV starts warning about them; the version and the build
time of the signatures come from VERSION, or from VERSIONCOMMANDS where
the configuration turns VERSION off.  With no clamd configured at all
the antivirus is not monitored, and a clamd that is configured but does
not answer is poor health.

WscGetSecurityProviderHealth gives the worst health of the providers
asked about; WscRegisterForChanges looks again every thirty seconds and
calls back when the health or the signatures' state changes.  The
product list (WSCProductList) lists ClamAV, as an antivirus and as an
antispyware product, with its name, state, signature status, when its
signatures were built as the product's timestamp, and a GUID of its
own.  There is no firewall product.

With clamd running Outlook no longer prompts; with
WINE_AMSI_CLAMD_SOCKET pointing nowhere it prompts again.

Not measured yet: the errors for NULL pointers, no provider and unknown
providers, a list used before Initialize or initialized twice, and an
item index past the end; the tests only print those.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

